      "fullText": "Defense Secretary Pete Hegseth used a &#8220; State of the Force &#8221; address on Wednesday to launch a new effort to study how war may be fought as tech advances rapidly. It&#8217;s being called Project Meridian. Elon Musk, Anduril founder Palmer Luckey, and former House Speaker Newt Gingrich will co-lead the project. They&#8217;ll be joined by others from fields such as academia and policy, all working under the direction of Defense Department CTO Emil Michael. Their job is to &#8220;study the battlefields of the future&#8221; and the &#8220;weapons and technologies warfighters&#8221; may use on them, said Hegseth in a letter announcing the project. The team (it is unclear how many people will be on it) will have 120 days to submit its findings and propose “actionable solutions to start developing, testing, and fielding” advanced technology meant to keep America a dominant military power, per Hegseth. Hegseth also announced an Autonomous Warfare Command on Wednesday to speed up the development and deployment of autonomous systems and drones. One curious detail in the Pentagon&#8217;s announcement is that the study will come through a partner organization , but the announcement does not say what that organization is or who leads it. After TechCrunch reached out to the Defense Department for comment, the department referred us to a press release , saying it had &#8220;nothing further to provide beyond the Secretary’s comments.&#8221; Musk&#8217;s and Luckey&#8217;s involvement is hardly surprising. Musk, in particular, has reportedly renewed close ties with the administration and recently had lunch with the president — alongside other top AI leaders — to discuss the future of AI . The project seemingly marks his first official return to the administration&#8217;s fold since he left the Department of Government Efficiency (DOGE) last year. Musk’s SpaceX launches satellites for the U.S. government and operates a satellite network that supports national defense and s"
